    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) DOH-COOL-02 DOH-Informed · Cooling Controls DOH Cooling & Reheating Department / Area DOH-Informed Cooling Controls Prepared By Manager / PIC Date Reviewed June 2026 Version 1.0 Detailed two-stage cooling, approved cooling methods, reheating limits, and discard rules. Key Actions Cool from 140°F to 70°F within 2 hours. Cool from 70°F to 41°F within 4 additional hours. Reheat food for hot holding to 165°F within 2 hours. Discard food when cooling or reheating limits cannot be proven. 1 Purpose Control one of the highest-risk points in food service: cooked food passing slowly through the temperature danger zone during cooling or reheating. 2 Scope Applies to DOH-Informed Cooling Controls and any staff member assigned to this process, task, equipment, checklist, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Manager / PIC) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. Food that requires time/temperature control for safety Food that must be controlled by time or temperature to limit pathogen growth or toxin production. HACCP Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Point; a written food safety plan required for certain specialized processes. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Two-stage cooling standard: 140°F to 70°F within 2 hours, then 70°F to 41°F within 4 additional hours. Total maximum cooling time is 6 hours. Food made from ingredients at room temperature must be cooled to 41°F or below within 4 hours. ROP foods have stricter cooling requirements and must meet the applicable 38°F and 34°F standards when used. Reheat previously cooked and cooled food that requires time/temperature control for safety for hot holding to 165°F within 2 hours. Never reheat in a steam table, soup warmer, or hot holding cabinet. Use a stove, oven, microwave, or other active cooking equipment. Do not cover hot food tightly while cooling. Cover only after food reaches 41°F or below, unless loosely protected from overhead contamination. Do not perform reduced oxygen packaging, cook-chill, or sous vide unless the restaurant has prior Department approval and an approved HACCP plan for that food/process. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) When To Check Any time cooked soups, sauces, rice, beans, meats, poultry, seafood, vegetables, or leftovers are cooled for later use. Any time hot food i"
