    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) DOH-HEALTH-05 DOH-Informed · Staff Controls DOH Employee Health & Hygiene Department / Area DOH-Informed Staff Controls Prepared By Manager / PIC Date Reviewed June 2026 Version 1.0 NYC DOH source rules for employee illness, handwashing, bare-hand contact, wounds, glove use, and hand sink access. Key Actions Do not allow food workers with listed restricted diseases, symptoms, or uncovered infected wounds to work in food operations. Wash hands before food work and after every contamination trigger. Prevent bare-hand contact with ready-to-eat food. Keep hand sinks within required access, stocked, unobstructed, and used only for handwashing. 1 Purpose Prevent foodborne illness caused by sick workers, poor handwashing, infected wounds, bare-hand contact, and blocked or misused hand sinks. 2 Scope Applies to DOH-Informed Staff Controls and any staff member assigned to this process, task, equipment, checklist, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Manager / PIC) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Do not allow a food worker to work if the worker has a food-related illness or a communicable disease listed in Article 81, including acute infectious diarrhea, amebiasis, cholera, cryptosporidiosis, diphtheria, E. coli O157:H7, giardiasis, hepatitis A, salmonellosis, shigellosis, streptococcal sore throat, tuberculosis, typhoid, or an infected cut or wound. Workers must report vomiting, diarrhea, fever, jaundice, sore throat with fever, infected cuts/boils, diagnosed foodborne illness, or exposure that may create a food safety risk. Hands and exposed arms must be washed before starting work and after toilet use, raw food handling, touching face/hair/body, coughing/sneezing, eating/drinking/smoking, handling garbage/chemicals, cleaning, using phones, handling money, changing tasks, or any possible contamination. Ready-to-eat food must be prepared and served without bare-hand contact unless the food will be heated to the required Article 81 minimum temperature. Use gloves, deli tissue, tongs, spatulas, or other barriers. Gloves are not a substitute for handwashing. Hands must be washed before putting on gloves and gloves must be changed whenever contaminated, torn, or when tasks change. Handwashing sinks must b"
