    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) DOH-INSP-08 DOH-Informed · Manager Routine DOH Inspection Readiness Department / Area DOH-Informed Manager Routine Prepared By Manager / PIC Date Reviewed June 2026 Version 1.0 Manager self-inspection routine organized around Article 81 critical areas and records inspectors commonly request. Key Actions Make sure a Food Protection Certificate holder is on premises and supervising food operations whenever required. Walk temperatures, hand sinks, warewashing, pests, source control, and food protection first. Keep FPC, pest contract, HACCP approvals, logs, shellfish tags, and corrective actions accessible. Fix critical items immediately and document what changed. 1 Purpose Prepare the restaurant to meet DOH expectations every day by turning inspection items into a repeatable manager routine. 2 Scope Applies to DOH-Informed Manager Routine and any staff member assigned to this process, task, equipment, checklist, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Manager / PIC) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. HACCP Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Point; a written food safety plan required for certain specialized processes. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed A Food Protection Certificate holder must be on premises and supervise all food preparation activities during all hours of operation when required by Article 81. Food Protection Certificate must be available for Department inspection. Pest management contract and service records must be available. Temperature logs, sanitizer logs, cooling logs, receiving records, shellfish tags, approved HACCP plans, and corrective actions must be current and accessible where used. Critical food temperature, illness, handwashing, bare-hand contact, warewashing, pest, unsafe source, ROP/HACCP, and potable water issues require immediate action. Suspected patron foodborne illness complaints and emergency occurrences must be escalated for required reporting and follow-up. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) When To Check Opening manager walk-through. Pre-service line check. Mid-service or shift-change spot check. Closing verification. Before expected inspections, after complaints, or after any major equipment/power/water failure. Step 1 - Confirm certified supervision Verify the Food Protection Certificate holder is"
