    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) DOH-THERM-03 DOH-Informed · Measuring Temperatures DOH Thermometer Control Department / Area DOH-Informed Measuring Temperatures Prepared By Manager / PIC Date Reviewed June 2026 Version 1.0 Thermometer availability, proper probe use, cleaning, calibration, and verification routines. Key Actions Keep an accurate probe thermometer available wherever food that requires time/temperature control for safety is handled. Clean and sanitize the probe before and after each use. Probe the thickest or coldest part of the food and wait for the reading to stabilize. Calibrate when dropped, after extreme temperature changes, or when accuracy is questionable. 1 Purpose Make sure food safety decisions are based on accurate product temperatures rather than guesses, surface readings, or equipment displays. 2 Scope Applies to DOH-Informed Measuring Temperatures and any staff member assigned to this process, task, equipment, checklist, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Manager / PIC) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. Food that requires time/temperature control for safety Food that must be controlled by time or temperature to limit pathogen growth or toxin production. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed An appropriately scaled metal-stem thermometer, thermocouple, or digital thermometer must be available and used for cooking, cooling, reheating, and holding checks. Glass thermometers are not allowed in food service operations. Bi-metallic stem thermometers must be inserted to the dimple or sensing area; digital/thermistor probes usually need about 3/4 inch inserted unless manufacturer instructions say otherwise. Equipment thermometers must be present in refrigerators, freezers, and hot holding units and placed where they reflect the warmest or most representative area. Ice-point calibration target is 32°F. Boiling-point calibration target is 212°F at sea level, adjusted for altitude if needed. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) When To Check At opening, before the first temperature log is completed. Before cooking, cooling, reheating, or holding decisions are accepted. After a thermometer is dropped, damaged, exposed to extreme heat/cold, or gives a suspicious reading. Whenever a manager reviews a temperature-related corrective action. Step 1 - Inspect th"
