    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) DOH-SANI-04 DOH-Informed · Cleaning & Sanitizing DOH Warewashing & Sanitizing Department / Area DOH-Informed Cleaning & Sanitizing Prepared By Manager / PIC Date Reviewed June 2026 Version 1.0 NYC DOH source limits for manual warewashing, mechanical warewashing, wiping cloths, and food-contact surfaces. Key Actions Manual warewashing sequence is wash, rinse, sanitize, air dry. Use the correct sanitizer strength for the method: immersion, wiping/spraying/pouring, wiping cloth storage, or dish machine. High-temperature mechanical dish machines must meet the required final rinse and utensil surface temperature standards. Never guess sanitizer strength by smell or color; use the correct test kit. 1 Purpose Prevent contamination from dirty utensils, food-contact surfaces, wiping cloths, cutting boards, reusable containers, and failed warewashing equipment. 2 Scope Applies to DOH-Informed Cleaning & Sanitizing and any staff member assigned to this process, task, equipment, checklist, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Manager / PIC) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Manual warewashing requires a three-compartment sink unless a specific exception applies. Items must be washed, rinsed, sanitized, and air dried. Hot-water manual sanitizing: immerse utensils in clean hot water at not less than 170°F for at least one-half minute. Chlorine soaking/immersion: 50 ppm for 1 minute at 75°F to 140°F, using the concentration required by the Food Protection manual and product label. Chlorine swabbing, wiping, spraying, or pouring: 100 ppm at 75°F to 140°F unless product label requires a different approved concentration. Moist wiping cloths must be stored in sanitizer between uses; the Food Protection manual states this solution must be at least 50 ppm and changed frequently. Iodine immersion target from the Food Protection manual: 12.5 ppm for 1 minute at 75°F or warmer, unless the product label requires otherwise. Quaternary ammonium compounds must be used according to manufacturer label directions for concentration and contact time. High-temperature mechanical dish machines must use a final rinse of at least 180°F and achieve a utensil surface temperature of at least 160°F. Low-temperature chemical dish machi"
