    "body": "HACCP Plan Manager / Head Chef Updated: May 2026 Category: Food Safety Hazard Analysis & Critical Control Points HACCP is the food safety management system required by law for all commercial food businesses. It identifies where food safety hazards can occur in our processes, establishes controls to prevent them, and defines what to do when those controls fail. This page is the working summary of our HACCP plan. All staff must understand the Critical Control Points (CCPs) that apply to their role. The 7 Principles 1. Hazard Analysis — Identify all biological, chemical, and physical hazards at each stage of food handling 2. Identify CCPs — Determine which steps are Critical Control Points where a hazard can be prevented or eliminated 3. Set Critical Limits — Define the min/max value (e.g., temperature) that must be met at each CCP 4. Monitoring — Establish how each CCP will be monitored, how often, and by whom 5. Corrective Actions — Define what to do when monitoring shows a CCP is out of control 6. Verification — Confirm the HACCP system is working — through audits, reviews, and testing 7. Record Keeping — Document all monitoring results, corrective actions, and verification activities Our Critical Control Points CCP 1 — Receiving Hazard: Pathogen growth, chemical contamination, or physical contamination in incoming goods Critical limit: Chilled goods 46 F on arrival. Frozen goods solidly frozen. No damaged packaging. Valid use-by dates present. Monitoring: Probe temperature of each chilled category on every delivery. Visual inspection of all packaging. Corrective action: Reject goods that do not meet receiving standards. Log rejection on Receiving Log. Record: Receiving Log — every delivery CCP 2 — Cold Storage Hazard: Pathogen growth due to temperature abuse in fridges and freezers Critical limit: Cold food 41 F. Frozen food 0 F. Hot food 140 F. Monitoring: Temperature check at opening, during service, and at closing. Probe check of a mid-shelf food item at opening. Corrective action: Food above 46 F — do not use. Alert manager. Arrange equipment repair. See Corrective Action SOP . Record: Daily Temperature Log CCP 3 — Cooking Hazard: Survival of pathogens due to insufficient cooking Critical limit: Poultry 165 F core. Fish 145 F core. Whole cuts of beef/lamb 145 F core. Minced meat 165 F core. Monitoring: Probe every portion of high-risk proteins at the time of cooking. Calibrated probe thermometer only. Corrective action: Return to heat until minimum temperature is reached. Do not serve. Log the corrective action. Record: Cook temperature noted on Daily Temperature Log where applicable CCP 4 — Cooling Hazard: Rapid pathogen growth during slow cooling of cooked food Critical limit: Cool from above 140 F to below 70 F within 2 hours, then to 41 F or below within 4 hours total. Monitoring: Probe food during cooling at 30-minute intervals. Record start temperature and times. Corrective action: Food not at 41 F within 4 hours — discard. Log the cor"
