    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example FOH Critical Front of House Allergen Communication Department / Area Front of House FOH Critical Prepared By FOH Manager, General Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Allergen Communication defines the required standard, sequence, and controls for this procedure. Key Actions Ask every table before ordering. Confirm with kitchen before promising. Enter the allergen in the POS. Announce allergen dishes at delivery. 1 Purpose This SOP defines the required standard for allergen communication so the team follows the same safe, consistent process every shift. 2 Scope Applies to FOH Staff and any team member assigned to this procedure, related checks, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (FOH Manager, General Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Step 1 - Ask every table before ordering Ask clearly: “Does anyone have any allergies or dietary restrictions we need to know about?” Pause and listen. Do not rush past the answer. If a guest says “a little” or “not serious,” still treat it as an allergy until a manager confirms otherwise. Step 2 - Confirm with kitchen before promising If a guest asks whether something is safe, say: “Let me confirm that with the kitchen.” Do not guess from memory. Check the recipe, check prep exposure, and confirm with the chef or manager before answering. The dangerous answer is “it should be fine.” Use confirmed facts only. Step 3 - Enter the allergen in the POS Add the allergen as a clear modifier on every relevant course. Use plain language: “ALLERGY: GLUTEN” or “ALLERGY: NUTS.” Do not bury allergy notes in vague comments. Tell the expo or chef verbally as well. Step 4 - Announce allergen dishes at delivery When the dish is delivered, say the dish name and allergen modification out loud: “Sea bass, gluten allergy confirmed.” Do not auction allergen food. Place it directly in front of the correct guest. 7 Safety & Compliance Considerations Corrective Action If the stand"
