    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example FOH Service Front of House Complaints & Recovery Department / Area Front of House FOH Service Prepared By FOH Manager, General Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Complaints & Recovery defines the required standard, sequence, and controls for this procedure. Key Actions Listen without defending. Identify the type of problem. Offer a clear next action. Record the check before the end of the shift. 1 Purpose This SOP defines the required standard for complaints & recovery so the team follows the same safe, consistent process every shift. 2 Scope Applies to Server / Manager and any team member assigned to this procedure, related checks, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (FOH Manager, General Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Step 1 - Listen without defending Stop what you are doing, face the guest, and let them explain. Do not interrupt, blame the kitchen, blame another server, or explain policy first. Use: “I’m sorry that happened. Let me fix it.” Step 2 - Identify the type of problem Separate the issue: food quality, timing, incorrect item, allergen concern, bill error, atmosphere, or staff behaviour. Food safety, allergy, injury, or harassment concerns go to the manager immediately. Step 3 - Offer a clear next action Tell the guest exactly what will happen next: remake, replacement, manager visit, bill correction, or time update. Give realistic timing. If you need approval, say so and get the manager now. Step 4 - Document before the shift ends Record table number, time, issue, action taken, comp or discount, and manager involved. Mention it in handover if the guest may contact the restaurant later. A complaint handled well can become loyalty. A complaint hidden from the manager becomes a repeat failure. 7 Safety & Compliance Considerations Corrective Action If the standard cannot be met Stop the task, tell the manager, isolate any affected food, equipment, or records, and follow the"
