    "body": "Bartender Your First Week Role: Bartender New Hire Path Updated: May 2026 This is your path through the first week behind the bar. It is not a general orientation — it is a day-by-day reading list specific to this role that tells you exactly what to read, in what order, before you work the bar alone. Follow it in sequence. Each SOP takes a few minutes to read and will make a real difference when service is moving fast and you cannot stop to ask. At the end of the week, you sign off to confirm you have read and understood everything. That sign-off goes to your manager. It is not a test. It is a record that you were given the information. Before Your First Shift Three reads. Fifteen minutes. Do this before you arrive. These three SOPs are the non-negotiable starting points for the bartender role. They cover hygiene, allergen communication, and the table service sequence. Allergens behind the bar are a real risk — nuts in cocktails, dairy in coffee, gluten in beer. Read these before you walk in. You will be expected to know them. Read in order 01 Handwashing Procedure ~2 min 02 Allergen Communication ~4 min 03 Table Service Sequence ~4 min By the time you arrive for your first shift, you should be able to name the Big 9 allergens, explain what steps to take when an allergy ticket comes in for a bar order, and understand the basic service sequence for drink orders made at the bar. Day One Shadow the bar. Learn the layout. Do not work a section alone. Your first shift is about orientation, not speed. You will shadow your trainer through the full service. Watch how the bar is set up before doors open, how tabs and table orders are managed in the POS, and how cash and card payments are handled. You are not expected to work independently. You are expected to be present, ask questions, and not improvise behind the bar. Confirm each of these with your trainer before service starts: Where everything lives behind the bar — spirits, modifiers, glassware, tools, and ice wells How to log into the bar POS, open a tab, and route a table order How an allergy flag is handled when a drink order comes in Where allergen information for cocktails, coffee, and beer is kept How cash is counted and reconciled — never alone, always with a witness What to do if a tap blows or a piece of equipment fails mid-service Who is shift lead today and when to escalate At the end of day one, you should know where everything lives, how the POS handles tabs and table orders, and what the correct allergen process looks like for a bar order. You do not need to be fast. You need to know the process. Day Two Learn the money and the difficult moments. Before your second shift, read these two SOPs. They cover payment and cash-up, and complaints and recovery. The bar handles more payment transactions than any other role on the floor — and cash errors are the ones that matter at end of shift. On day two you will begin to assist with orders under supervision. You are not working the bar independ"
