    "body": "Host Your First Week Role: Host New Hire Path Updated: May 2026 This is your path through the first week as a host. It is not a general orientation — it is a day-by-day reading list specific to this role that tells you exactly what to read, in what order, before you own the door alone. Follow it in sequence. Each SOP takes a few minutes to read and will make the difference between a smooth service and a chaotic one. At the end of the week, you sign off to confirm you have read and understood everything. That sign-off goes to your manager. It is not a test. It is a record that you were given the information. Before Your First Shift Three reads. Fifteen minutes. Do this before you arrive. These three SOPs are the non-negotiable starting points for the host role. They cover hygiene, allergen communication, and the greeting and seating sequence. The host is the first person every guest interacts with — and the handoff you make at the table carries that guest's allergy information into the kitchen. Read these before your first shift. You will be expected to know them. Read in order 01 Handwashing Procedure ~2 min 02 Allergen Communication ~4 min 03 Greeting & Seating ~4 min By the time you arrive for your first shift, you should know the correct greeting sequence, understand why the allergy handoff at the table is your responsibility — not the guest's — and be able to describe what happens when a guest with an allergy is seated. Day One Shadow the door. Watch the flow. Do not seat a table alone. Your first shift is about orientation, not ownership. You will shadow your trainer through the full service — from opening the entrance and checking the room, through the service rush, to closing the host stand. Watch how reservations are read before service, how the seating plan is managed under pressure, and how the team communicates. You are not expected to work independently. You are expected to be attentive and ask questions. Confirm each of these with your trainer before service starts: How to open the reservations system and where today's bookings are displayed How to read the seating chart and understand which sections belong to which server Where allergy notes appear in the reservation system and how they are communicated at handoff How to walk a guest to a table — not point and direct What to say when quoting a walk-in wait time Where fire exits, restrooms, and accessibility routes are Who is shift lead today and when to escalate At the end of day one, you should know the room layout, how the reservation system works at a basic level, and what the correct allergy handoff looks like at the table. You do not need to be fast. You need to know the process. Day Two Learn how the booking system and guest recovery work. Before your second shift, read these two SOPs. They cover reservations and waitlist management, and complaints and recovery. On day two you will begin to assist with seating guests under supervision. You are not running the door independentl"
