    "body": "Prep Cook Your First Week Role: Prep Cook New Hire Path Updated: May 2026 Prep sets up everything that service depends on. If prep is done wrong — wrong portions, missing labels, cross-contaminated boards — the problem doesn't show up until the kitchen is full and there's no time to fix it. This path gives you what you need to do the job correctly from the start. Read each SOP before the shift it belongs to. At the end of the week, complete the sign-off form. That form goes to your manager and confirms you were given the information. Before Your First Shift Three reads before you arrive. Hygiene, knives, and allergens are the three areas that cause serious problems when someone gets them wrong on day one. Read these before you walk in. You will be expected to know them from the moment you start. Read in order 01 Handwashing Procedure ~2 min 02 Knife Safety & Storage ~3 min 03 Allergen Handling ~4 min Before you arrive, you should be able to name the Big 9 allergens, explain why separate cutting boards matter, and know how to pass a knife safely. These are not optional — they apply on day one. Day One Learn the walk-in and how things are labelled before you touch anything. Your first shift is orientation. You will shadow your trainer through the prep session. Watch how items are pulled from the walk-in, how boards are assigned, and how prepped containers are labelled before they go back. Do not start any task until your trainer has shown you how it is done here — methods vary between kitchens and what worked somewhere else may not be how we do it. By the end of day one, confirm you know all of these with your trainer: What goes on a label — contents, prep date, use-by date, and who made it Which cutting board color is used for which food type Where the color-coded boards are stored and how they are cleaned between tasks How stock is rotated in the walk-in — oldest to the front, every time How to handle an unlabelled container you find in the walk-in Where allergen information is posted and what to do if you are not sure about an ingredient You are not expected to work at full speed today. You are expected to observe correctly and ask questions when something is unclear. Silence is not the right answer when you don't understand something. Day Two Station setup and storage — the two systems you run every day. Before your second shift, read these two SOPs. On day two you will begin working through tasks with your trainer watching. You will be expected to set up the prep station correctly, apply labels accurately, and return items to the walk-in in the right order. Your trainer will check each of these before stepping back. Read before this shift 04 Cold Prep Setup ~4 min 05 Dry Storage ~3 min By the end of day two, your trainer should be able to step away and trust that your station is set up correctly and your labels are right. If they cannot, that conversation will happen before day three. Day Three Onward The checklist is your accountability for e"
