    "body": "Server Your First Week Role: Server New Hire Path Updated: May 2026 This is your path through the first week as a server. It is not a general orientation — it is a day-by-day reading list specific to this role that tells you exactly what to read, in what order, before you take a table alone. Follow it in sequence. Each SOP takes a few minutes to read and will make your first shifts significantly less stressful. At the end of the week, you sign off to confirm you have read and understood everything. That sign-off goes to your manager. It is not a test. It is a record that you were given the information. Before Your First Shift Three reads. Fifteen minutes. Do this before you arrive. These three SOPs are the non-negotiable starting points for this role. They cover hygiene, allergen communication, and the table service sequence — the three areas where a mistake in the first week causes a real problem for a guest. Read them before you walk in. You will be expected to know them from the start. Read in order 01 Handwashing Procedure ~2 min 02 Allergen Communication ~4 min 03 Table Service Sequence ~4 min By the time you arrive for your first shift, you should be able to explain how an allergy is entered in the POS and confirmed with the kitchen, and know the correct sequence for greeting, taking orders, and closing a table. Day One Shadow the section. Observe everything. Do not take a table alone. Your first shift is about orientation, not output. You will shadow your trainer through the full service. Watch how the section is set up before doors open, how orders move from table to POS to kitchen, and how the team communicates during a rush. You are not expected to work independently. You are expected to be attentive, ask questions when you do not follow something, and not improvise. Confirm each of these with your trainer before service starts: Where your section is and how it is laid out on the floor plan How to log into the POS and how to select a table Where allergen information for every dish is kept and how to flag a guest allergy How a guest allergy is entered in the POS and communicated to the kitchen Where the water station, linen, and cutlery are stocked for your section How to fire a course and how to call away Who is shift lead today and how to reach them during service At the end of day one, you should know where everything is, how the POS works at a basic level, and how allergen flags are handled. You do not need to be fast. You need to know the process. Day Two Learn how to handle the difficult moments. Before your second shift, read these two SOPs. They cover complaints and recovery, and payment and cash-up — the two areas new servers most often get wrong because they are uncomfortable situations, not technical ones. On day two you will begin to assist with tables under supervision. You will not be running a full section independently yet, but you will be expected to understand what the correct response is before you are in it. Read befo"
