    "body": "Braised Beef Short Rib Role: Line Cook Updated: May 2026 Category: Mains Yield 8 portions Portion 1 rib · 280 g Plating time ~3 min Braise time 3.5 hrs Minimum safe reheat temperature: 165 F throughout. Probe the thickest part of every rib before service. This dish is braised in advance and reheated to order — the probe check in Step 5 is mandatory, not optional. Contains Celery Gluten Milk Sulphites Ingredients For the braise 8 Bone-in beef short ribs Bembridges Butchery — English, bone-in, approx. 300 g each before trim 500 ml Red wine Côtes du Rhône or similar — no cooking wine 1 L Beef stock House-made dark stock — prep fridge, labelled 2 sticks Celery, rough-chopped 2 Carrots, rough-chopped 1 Brown onion, halved 4 cloves Garlic, crushed 2 tbsp Tomato paste 2 sprigs Thyme 1 Bay leaf to season Flaky sea salt and cracked black pepper For the creamed potato 1.2 kg Maris Piper potatoes, peeled and quartered 120 g Unsalted butter, cold and cubed Lurpak unsalted — or Charentes-Poitou if in stock 180 ml Double cream, warmed to season Fine salt and white pepper To plate 40 ml per plate Braising jus, reduced and strained (from the braise) 3–4 sprigs Watercress, washed and dried Vitacress — check garnish fridge pinch Flaky sea salt to finish — Maldon Step 1 Season and sear the ribs Pat each rib completely dry with paper towel — surface moisture is the enemy of a good crust. Season generously on all sides with flaky salt and cracked black pepper. Heat a heavy-based pan over high heat until smoking. Sear each rib 2–3 minutes per side until deeply browned on all surfaces including the ends. Work in batches — crowding steams rather than sears. Transfer to a deep roasting tray. The color you build here becomes the flavour of the finished sauce. Pale ribs going into the braise means a flat jus at the end. Step 2 Build the braise In the same pan over medium heat, sweat the onion, carrot, celery, and garlic until softened — about 8 minutes. Add tomato paste and cook for 2 minutes until it darkens slightly. Deglaze with the red wine, scraping up all the fond from the base. Reduce by half. Pour over the ribs in the tray. Add stock until the ribs are three-quarters submerged. Tuck in the thyme and bay leaf. Step 3 Braise covered at 300°F for 3.5 hours Cover the tray tightly with a double layer of foil. Place in the oven at 300°F fan. Do not open for the first 3 hours. After 3 hours, check — the meat should yield when pressed with a spoon and the bone should move freely at the thin end. If not, return for 30 more minutes. When done, rest in the liquid for 20 minutes before handling. Ribs can be braised the day before service and held overnight submerged in their liquid in the fridge. Reheat covered at 325°F for 25 minutes before service. Step 4 Reduce the jus and make the creamed potato Carefully lift the ribs from the liquid and set aside. Strain the braising liquid through a fine sieve into a wide saucepan, pressing the vegetables to extract everything. Reduce o"
