    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example Hygiene Back of House Color Coding System Department / Area Back of House Hygiene Prepared By Chef, Kitchen Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Use the correct color-coded board, utensil, and storage position every time so raw proteins, allergens, and ready-to-eat foods do not cross-contaminate. Key Actions Choose one color for the task. Follow the fridge storage hierarchy. Wash and sanitize between uses. Store boards in designated positions. 1 Purpose This SOP explains the kitchen color-coding system for cutting boards, utensils, prep surfaces, and fridge storage. The goal is to prevent cross-contamination at the source. 2 Scope Applies to all kitchen staff handling raw meat, poultry, fish, seafood, produce, dairy, cooked food, ready-to-eat food, or allergen-sensitive prep. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Chef, Kitchen Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Color Reference Color Use For Red Raw red meat: beef, lamb, pork, venison Yellow Raw poultry: chicken, duck, turkey Blue Raw fish and seafood Green Fresh produce, fruit, salad, and vegetables White Dairy, bread, pastry, and cooked ready-to-eat foods Purple / Brown Allergen-dedicated prep with no cross-contact Critical Rule If the correct color is not available, stop and tell the manager. Do not substitute another color because service is busy. Step 1 Choose one color for the task Match the board and utensils to the food category before prep starts. If you start a task on a red board, complete that raw red meat task on the red board. Do not move food from one color to another. Step 2 Follow the fridge storage hierarchy Store ready-to-eat foods above raw proteins. Raw poultry always goes on the bottom shelf. All raw proteins must be covered, sealed, and stored where drip cannot reach food below. Step 3 Wash and sanitize between uses Wash boards and utensils with hot soapy water, rinse, and sanitize before switching tasks, even within the same color catego"
