    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example Food Safety Back of House Cooling & Reheating Department / Area Back of House Food Safety Prepared By Chef, Kitchen Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Improper cooling is the leading cause of foodborne illness in commercial kitchens. Hot food left to cool slowly passes through the danger zone (41°F–140°F) for too long, allowing bacteria to multiply rapidly. Cool fast, label always, reheat to piping hot — every time. Key Actions Divide into shallow portions immediately. Cool using an ice bath or blast chiller. Check temperature and refrigerate within 90 minutes. Label with date, time, and use-by. Reheat rapidly to 165°F throughout. 1 Purpose This SOP defines the required standard for cooling & reheating so the team follows the same safe, consistent process every shift. 2 Scope Applies to Kitchen Staff and any team member assigned to this procedure, related checks, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Chef, Kitchen Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Reference Cooling Reheating Temperature Quick Reference Stage Target Cool to (stage 1) < 70 F within 2 hrs Cool to (stage 2) 41 F within 4 hrs total Danger zone 41 F 140 F Reheat minimum 165 F throughout Step 1 - Divide into shallow portions immediately As soon as a batch of food finishes cooking, divide it into shallow containers no deeper than 7 cm. Large, deep containers retain heat in the centre and will not cool fast enough. Transfer to gastronorm trays, deli containers, or hotel pans — do not leave food cooling in the pot it was cooked in. Note — Larger joints or whole proteins should be portioned or sliced before cooling. The goal is to maximise surface area so heat escapes quickly. Step 2 - Cool using an ice bath or blast chiller Place shallow containers into an ice bath — a larger container or the prep sink filled with ice and cold water. Stir the food regularly to release heat from the centre. Alternatively use the blast chiller if "
