    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example Food Safety Back of House Corrective Action Department / Area Back of House Food Safety Prepared By Chef, Kitchen Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 When a food safety control fails, stop the affected food or process, assess the risk, fix the root cause, and document the action before the end of the shift. Key Actions Stop and isolate. Assess the risk. Fix the root cause. Record the action before the end of the shift. 1 Purpose This SOP explains what to do when a food safety control measure fails, including temperature, sanitizer, labelling, storage, allergen, illness, pest, or equipment controls. 2 Scope Applies to all kitchen staff, managers, and any team member who identifies a failed food safety control during receiving, prep, service, closing, cleaning, or storage. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Chef, Kitchen Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. HACCP Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Point; a written food safety plan required for certain specialized processes. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Common Triggers Fridge or freezer temperature out of safe range Food not cooled to 41 F within the required time Reheated food not reaching 165 F Sanitizer concentration below or above required range Unlabelled or out-of-date food found in storage Raw protein stored above ready-to-eat food Allergen-modified dish prepared without clean board or tools Sick staff member reporting for a shift while excluded Pest evidence found in food storage or preparation areas Critical Rule Do not ignore the failure and continue service. Stop, isolate, assess, act, and document. Step 1 — Stop and isolate Stop using the affected food, equipment, or process immediately. Move food off the line or away from prep and label it \"ON HOLD - DO NOT USE\" until a manager has assessed it. Step 2 — Assess the risk A manager must assess how long the control failed, what temperature was reached, what food was affected, and whether the food can be safely used, reworked, re"
