    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example Storage Back of House Date Labelling & Food Rotation Department / Area Back of House Storage Prepared By Chef, Kitchen Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Unlabelled food is a fail during any health inspection. Every item in the fridge, freezer, and dry store that has been opened, prepped, or portioned must carry a label showing what it is, when it was made or opened, and when it must be used by. No exceptions. Key Actions Label every prepped item at the time of preparation. Apply the correct shelf-life by food type. Rotate stock using FIFO — First In, First Out. Check dates at the start of every shift. 1 Purpose This SOP defines the required standard for date labelling & food rotation so the team follows the same safe, consistent process every shift. 2 Scope Applies to All Kitchen Staff and any team member assigned to this procedure, related checks, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Chef, Kitchen Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Reference Max Shelf Life at 41 F Cooked proteins (chicken, beef, lamb, pork) 3 days Cooked fish and shellfish 2 days Stocks, sauces, and soups 3 days Raw marinated proteins 2 days Cut raw produce (salads, veg) 2 days Dairy-based sauces and desserts 2 days Anything without a label Discard immediately Note — These are maximum safe storage times. Sensory checks (smell, color, texture) should still be applied on each use, regardless of the use-by date. Step 1 - Label every prepped item at the time of preparation The label goes on before the container goes into storage — not later, not at the end of the shift. Labels must be written clearly in permanent marker on blue food-safe masking tape or printed adhesive labels. If you cannot read the label from arm's length, rewrite it. Every label must include three pieces of information: the item name, the date prepared, and the use-by date. Note — \"Sauce\" is not sufficient. Write the full name: \"Chicken Jus\" or \"Carro"
