    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example Storage Back of House Dry Storage Department / Area Back of House Storage Prepared By Chef, Kitchen Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Dry Storage defines the required standard, sequence, and controls for this procedure. Key Actions Store nothing on the floor — 15 cm minimum clearance. Label everything that leaves its original packaging. Rotate stock — first in, first out. 1 Purpose This SOP defines the required standard for dry storage so the team follows the same safe, consistent process every shift. 2 Scope Applies to All Kitchen Staff and any team member assigned to this procedure, related checks, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Chef, Kitchen Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Step 1 - Store nothing on the floor — 15 cm minimum clearance All dry goods must be stored on shelving at least 15 centimetres (6 inches) off the floor. This is a legal requirement, not a tidiness preference. It allows cleaning access underneath and protects stock from pest activity and moisture. Any items found on the floor during an opening check go on the shelf immediately. Note — Items on the floor is one of the top triggers for a failed environmental health inspection. Zero exceptions. Step 2 - Label everything that leaves its original packaging Any dry ingredient decanted into a container — flour, sugar, spices, dried pulses — must be labelled with the product name and date decanted. Use waterproof labels. This applies even if the container has a clear lid and the contents look obvious. Over a busy service, assumptions cause mistakes. Step 3 - Rotate stock — first in, first out When restocking dry goods from a delivery, move older stock to the front of the shelf before placing new items behind it. Check the use-by or best-before date on both old and new stock. If new stock has an earlier use-by than existing stock, place it in front. This is FIFO — First In, First Out — and it applies to every shel"
