    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example Hygiene All Staff Employee Health Policy Department / Area All Departments Hygiene Prepared By Manager, Kitchen Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Report illness before entering food-handling areas so guests, coworkers, and the business are protected from foodborne illness risk. Key Actions Exclude staff with reportable symptoms. Cover wounds with approved waterproof dressings. Send staff home if symptoms start mid-shift. Report illness after food handling immediately. Keep excluded staff out of food areas. 1 Purpose This SOP explains when staff must stay home, when symptoms must be reported to a manager, and what is required before returning to work after illness. 2 Scope Applies to all staff, managers, contractors, and anyone entering kitchen, prep, dish, storage, service, or guest-facing work areas. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Manager, Kitchen Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Exclusion Do not come to work if you have any of the following. Call your manager as early as possible before your shift. Vomiting or diarrhoea in the last 48 hours Jaundice, including yellowing of the skin or eyes Diagnosed or suspected Norovirus, Salmonella, E. coli O157, Shigella, or Hepatitis A Persistent unexplained high fever Return Limit You must be symptom-free for at least 48 hours after the last episode of vomiting or diarrhoea before returning to work. Restriction Report these symptoms before the shift starts. A manager will decide whether you can work, need restricted duties, or must go home. Sore throat with fever Infected wound, boil, or skin lesion on hands, wrists, or face Persistent sneezing or runny nose with direct food contact risk Persistent cough that cannot be controlled Wounds Infected cuts or wounds on hands or wrists must be covered with a blue detectable plaster before food handling, with gloves worn over it. Open wounds that cannot be fully covered are excluded from food handling. Reporting Pro"
