    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example Equipment Back of House Fryer Startup Department / Area Back of House Equipment Prepared By Chef, Kitchen Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Fryer Startup defines the required standard, sequence, and controls for this procedure. Key Actions Check oil level before switching on. Set temperature dial to service setting. Confirm temperature with probe thermometer. Assess oil quality — color and smell. 1 Purpose This SOP defines the required standard for fryer startup so the team follows the same safe, consistent process every shift. 2 Scope Applies to Line Cook / Opener and any team member assigned to this procedure, related checks, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Chef, Kitchen Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Step 1 - Check oil level before switching on Look at the oil level in the fryer basket. It must sit between the MIN and MAX markers on the inside of the tank — never below minimum. Running a fryer with insufficient oil will damage the heating element and creates a fire risk. If oil is low, top up with fresh oil before proceeding. Note — Use the same oil type as what's in the tank. Do not mix oil types without checking with a supervisor. Step 2 - Set temperature dial to service setting Turn the temperature dial to 350 F — our standard service temperature for all fried items unless a specific item card says otherwise. The pilot light will activate. Do not use the fryer until the temperature indicator shows the oil has reached operating temperature. Note — Allow 15 20 minutes for the fryer to reach full temperature from cold. Set it at the start of opening prep, not when service is about to start. Step 3 - Confirm temperature with probe thermometer Once the indicator light signals the fryer is ready, confirm oil temperature with a calibrated probe thermometer. Dip only the tip — 3 to 4 centimetres — into the oil for 10 seconds. Reading must show 340 355 F before any food goes in."
