    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example Equipment Back of House Grill Cleaning Department / Area Back of House Equipment Prepared By Chef, Kitchen Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Grill Cleaning defines the required standard, sequence, and controls for this procedure. Key Actions Scrape the grill while still hot — end of service. Turn off and allow to cool before deep clean. Apply grill cleaner and scrub the grates. Season the grates before closing. 1 Purpose This SOP defines the required standard for grill cleaning so the team follows the same safe, consistent process every shift. 2 Scope Applies to Line Cook / Closer and any team member assigned to this procedure, related checks, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Chef, Kitchen Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Step 1 - Scrape the grill while still hot — end of service Immediately after the last ticket, while the grill is still at temperature, use the long-handled grill brush to scrape the grates from back to front. Work in firm parallel strokes — don't scrub back and forth. Debris falls into the drip tray below. Do not let the grill cool before scraping; baked-on carbon is far harder to remove. Note — Use heat-resistant gloves. The grill surface will be 480 F or above at end of service. Step 2 - Turn off and allow to cool before deep clean Switch the grill off at the control panel and allow it to cool to a safe handling temperature — below 140 F. This takes 20 to 30 minutes depending on the unit. Do not apply cleaning chemicals to a hot grill surface. Use this time to clean the surrounding station and empty the drip tray into the waste bin. Step 3 - Apply grill cleaner and scrub the grates Spray grill cleaner across the grate surface and leave to dwell for the time specified on the bottle — typically 3 to 5 minutes. Using a grill stone or heavy-duty scourer, scrub grates in the direction of the bars. Rinse thoroughly with hot water. No chemical residue should remain "
