    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example Hygiene All Staff Handwashing Procedure Department / Area All Departments Hygiene Prepared By Manager, Kitchen Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Wash hands at the right moments and with the correct technique so food, equipment, guests, and coworkers are protected from contamination. Key Actions Wet hands with warm running water. Apply soap and lather for 20 seconds. Rinse thoroughly. Dry with a single-use paper towel. 1 Purpose This SOP explains when staff must wash their hands and the exact method required at every designated handwashing sink. 2 Scope Applies to all staff working in food preparation, dishwashing, service, storage, receiving, cleaning, and guest-facing areas. Gloves, utensils, and sanitizer do not replace handwashing. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Manager, Kitchen Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Designated handwashing sink with warm running water Antibacterial hand soap Single-use paper towels Waste bin for used towels 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) When to Wash Wash hands every time one of these triggers happens, even if you washed recently. Before starting work and at the start of every shift Before handling ready-to-eat food After handling raw meat, poultry, fish, or eggs in the shell After touching your face, nose, mouth, hair, phone, tablet, or shared device After blowing your nose, sneezing, coughing, eating, drinking, or taking a break After removing gloves After handling waste, bins, dirty dishes, money, receipts, or cleaning chemicals After using the toilet After touching any surface that may be contaminated, including door handles and equipment controls Glove Rule Wash hands before putting gloves on and after taking gloves off. Contaminated gloves can transfer contamination the same way bare hands can. Step 1 Wet hands with warm running water Use only the designated handwashing sink. Wet hands thoroughly with warm, comfortable running water. Remove jewellery from hands and wrists before beginning. Step 2 Apply soap and lather for 20 seconds Apply a full pump of antibacterial soap. Lather palms, backs of hands, between fingers, under nails, thumbs, and wrists for at least 20 seconds. Step 3 Rinse thoroughly Rinse under running water until all soap is remov"
