    "body": "Staff Ready Ops. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) SOP Example Dishwashing Back of House Three-Sink Sanitizing Department / Area Back of House Dishwashing Prepared By Chef, Kitchen Manager Date Reviewed May 2026 Version 1.0 Three-Sink Sanitizing defines the required standard, sequence, and controls for this procedure. Key Actions Set up wash, rinse, and sanitize sinks. Wash until all soil is removed. Rinse with clean water. Sanitize for the full contact time. 1 Purpose This SOP defines the required standard for three-sink sanitizing so the team follows the same safe, consistent process every shift. 2 Scope Applies to Dishwasher and any team member assigned to this procedure, related checks, or manager follow-up. 3 Responsibilities All assigned staff must follow this SOP exactly as written during the applicable task or shift. The responsible manager or team (Chef, Kitchen Manager) is responsible for training staff, answering questions, and verifying that the procedure is followed. The shift lead or manager must stop the task and start corrective action when the standard cannot be met. 4 Definitions / Key Terms Term Definition SOP Standard Operating Procedure; the approved method for completing this task. Person in Charge The manager or certified lead responsible for supervising the task and making food safety decisions during the shift. Corrective Action The required response when a step, limit, record, or safety standard is missed or cannot be verified. 5 Materials / Equipment Needed Approved tools, equipment, utensils, and supplies required for the task. Required personal protective equipment, cleaning supplies, labels, logs, or checklists. Working hand sink, sanitizer, thermometer, or other food safety control equipment when applicable. 6 Procedure (Step-by-Step) Step 1 - Set up wash, rinse, and sanitize sinks Fill the first sink with hot water and detergent, the second sink with clean rinse water, and the third sink with sanitizer mixed to the label direction. Keep the sinks clearly separated so dirty water does not enter the sanitizer sink. Step 2 - Wash until all soil is removed Scrub the item fully in the wash sink, including handles, rims, corners, and undersides. Change wash water when it becomes greasy, cloudy, or cool. Sanitizer will not work properly on food residue or heavy grease. Step 3 - Rinse with clean water Move the item to the rinse sink and remove all soap. Do not skip this step. Detergent left on a surface can weaken sanitizer and leave residue on equipment that touches food. Step 4 - Sanitize for the full contact time Submerge the item fully in sanitizer for the contact time listed on the chemical label. Use test strips at the start of setup and whenever sanitizer is changed. If the strip does not read in the approved range, remake the sanitizer before continuing. Note Do not rinse sanitizer off after the contact time. Let the item air dry. 7 Safety & Compliance Considerations Corrective Action If the standard cannot be met"
